title: Birth
pid: d611f3c6-a49a-4be1-a810-2f4625251f3c
body: |2-
  The letter of a relation that put this occurrence here is shown low;
  its counts keep their own styles. An occurrence can have more than one
  birth relation. A side whose only members are ancestors that the birth
  relation already accounts for has tier low: =aC= (the parent is the sole
  container) shows the 'a' low. In the forks folder, whose viewparent
  is each member's grandparent 'b', the heralds tell the sides apart:
  a forkee shows =bK= (b forks it), with any other forkers counted
  before the letter (=2bK=: b and one more fork it), and the 'b' medium,
  because of b's floor; a forker-only member shows =Kb= (it forks b),
  with any other forkees counted before the 'b' too (=K2b=: it forks b
  and one more; on either side the numeral precedes the ancestor flags,
  as in =C9a=); a mutual shows =bKb= (=bK2b= when it forks one more).
  An indirect member, a mirror of b joined to it only through others
  (=docs/forks.org=, "Every mirror, flat"), shows neither 'b' flag,
  since it neither forks b nor is forked by it: =K1= for one that
  forks one note, say. The birth letter follows the side that stands
  as a member: =(forks in 2)= on a member that claims, =(forks out 2)=
  on one that forks b, both on a mutual, none on an indirect member.

  Immediately after =K=, a crucial =ĥ= (precomposed h with circumflex)
  means this occurrence stands in the place of one of its mirrors. =2Kĥ3= is an
  example. It comes from the load-bearing 'inPlaceOf' part; an
  occurrence without that fact has no =ĥ=. The
  eighth ancestor is a plain =h=, so =Kĥh= shows the two glyphs together.
  The fact preserves the original member ID when a substituted view
  is saved. See [[id:e6bc021f-1cb8-4ab0-9a5f-53874e60e14b][mirror substitution]].
